notes:
Boustrophedon: seven blocks turned (five _bits, four _rot), 92x153 -> 92x128.
Area x0.700, ticks x0.925, score x0.647 — both axes, because a row that stops existing is also a
row nobody walks. Ticks fell 7.4% on the phase-2/3 pair alone since those rows are inside the
per-lap walk and there are 4,096 laps; emit's rows are walked once and were near-pure area.

A westbound block is the eastbound block rotated 180 degrees, not mirrored: X/x/d/a keep their
chirality (relative to heading) and only > < ^ v swap. Three constraints the substrate charged:
  - The rotation axis is PER BLOCK. _bits touches no pipe so any axis draws it; _rot reads and
    writes ring V, and rotating it about 46 put those ops on columns 25-26, one past the v band's
    east edge, where they bind to ring B. A mis-bound ring op is invisible in the grid and in the
    answer's shape — only its value is wrong. _op now refuses it at build time.
  - Two-band blocks are unturnable (io-band ops move east, v-band ops west, rotation swaps them):
    _init, _leftvals, _rightvals, _tail, _peel_emit, _nosol.
  - Two turned blocks cannot be chained: one leaves heading west at column 23, the next is entered
    heading west at column 25, which is behind him. E3 pays a row going back east to turn again.

This finally beats the live 13,590,095,198, whose grid was never committed and could not be found
in any branch or worktree. Our subset-sum is now reproducible from the repo.
Remaining: ~10-14 more rows of the same transform (P2_ROT, P3_SKIP, _peel_sum at P2_PEEL/P3_PEEL,
seven v-only head rows) -> ~92x115, ~9.5e9. Sorted ring B is NOT worth it: its +14 rows are a
fixed cost, so a shorter room makes it a larger relative penalty (x0.845 at 153 rows, x0.874 at
128) — it never stops being eaten.
